<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Lyndon B. Johnson</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1908">1908</a>–<a href="/years/1973">1973</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Lyndon Baines Johnson, LBJ</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Lyndon Baines Johnson was the 36th president of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, serving from 1963 to 1969, who as vice president assumed the presidency after the assassination of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/john-f-kennedy">John F. Kennedy</a>. A Southern Democrat from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a>, he served in both houses of Congress and became Senate majority leader in 1954. His Great Society programmes and War on Poverty created Medicare and Medicaid, and he signed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/civil-rights-act-of-1964">Civil Rights Act of 1964</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/voting-rights-act-of-1965">Voting Rights Act of 1965</a> and the Civil Rights Act of 1968. He also escalated US involvement in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vietnam-war">Vietnam War</a>, and after disappointing results in the 1968 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/new-hampshire">New Hampshire</a> primary he withdrew his candidacy; he retired to his Texas ranch and died in 1973.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/lyndon-b-johnson.jpg" alt="Lyndon B. Johnson" width="330" height="436" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arnold Newman · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lyndon_B._Johnson,_photo_portrait,_color_(3x4_cropped)(2).jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>After Kennedy’s assassination President <b>Lyndon B. Johnson</b> drove the bill past a Senate filibuster of more than two months and signed the Civil Rights Act of 1964 on 2 July.</blockquote><a href="/movement/civil-rights-movement">Civil rights movement · Course of Events</a></li><li><blockquote>In the United States Franklin D. Roosevelt’s New Deal and <b>Lyndon B. Johnson</b>’s Great Society, with Medicare, Medicaid and the Civil Rights Act of 1964, expanded the welfare state.</blockquote><a href="/ideology/liberalism">Liberalism · In Power</a></li><li><blockquote>Andy Williams recorded it at Robert Kennedy's funeral in 1968, and it was sung at the burial of <b>Lyndon B. Johnson</b> in 1973 and at Jimmy Carter's funeral ceremony in 2025.</blockquote><a href="/songs/battle-hymn-of-the-republic">Battle Hymn of the Republic</a></li></ul></section>
